**How to read these numbers:**
- **Genres overlap, so the totals don't add up to 100%.** A game can belong to several genres at once; for example, a game can be both an ActionRPG and a DynamicAdventure. Its revenue counts in full for each genre it belongs to. Each percentage is that genre's share of all digital revenue, but the five together come to more than 100%.
- **Only confirmed genre matches are counted.** A game is only placed in a genre if it fully meets that genre's rules. Partial matches are left out, which keeps the genres clean but may miss some revenue.
- **This covers digital game sales only.** Hardware and subscription revenue aren't tied to specific games, so they can't be split by genre.
- **How sales were matched to genres:** each sale is linked to its game through the product sold. Game-sales titles were then matched to the game catalog, where several titles can point to the same catalog game. I added up revenue per title first, before matching to genres, so no sale is counted twice within a genre.

